<body>

<div class="header">
  <div class="logo">40th<br>Anniversary<br>1974–2014<br>Fielding<br>Graduate<br>University<br>Preparing Leaders<br>for a Changing World</div>
  <div class="header-text">
    <h1>Cigarette Smoking as a Mediator of a Relationship Between<br>Physical and Mental Health of the U.S. Immigrants</h1>
    <h2>Iya Ritchie, M.A.</h2>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="columns">

  <!-- Column 1: Introduction -->
  <div class="col">
    <div class="section-title">INTRODUCTION</div>
    <ul>
      <li>The immigrant paradox – immigrants frequently have better physical and mental health than the U.S.– born peers (Breslau et al., 2009)</li>
      <li>Presence of health and mental health problems were found among the U.S. immigrants (Montgomery et al., 2014; Read &amp; Barbosa, 2017)</li>
      <li>Connection between physical health (PH) and mental health (MH) has been well documented, but our understanding of their exchange is inadequate</li>
      <li>Objective: investigate direct and indirect associations between PH and MH (depression) in the U.S. immigrants. Hypotheses: 1) relationship between PH and MH is bidirectional and positive; 2) lifestyle choices and social engagement mediate PH-MH relationship; 3) immigrants' gender moderates PH-MH interactions</li>
      <li>Method: mediational analysis of archival data using a subsample of adults (n = 1,008) reporting health issues and depression on the New Immigrant Survey. Secondary analysis: PH-MH by gender</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="sub-heading">Findings for General sample (N = 1,008)</div>
    <ul>
      <li>MH significantly predicted PH (b = 0.16, p &lt; .001), mediation model explained 20.5% of PH</li>
      <li>Smoking mediated MH effect on PH (b = − 0.04), when MH = ≥ 5 symptoms</li>
      <li>Direct effect of PH on MH (b = 0.19, p &lt; .001) was observed, but not mediation</li>
      <li>Exercising and social engagement - no effect</li>
      <li>Older ag was predictive of physical symptoms but was protecting against depression</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="sub-heading">Findings for Males (n = 369) and Females (n = 619)</div>
    <ul>
      <li>Gender moderated PH–MH relationships: associations were significantly stronger in females</li>
      <li>Males: no significant associations between PH and MH</li>
      <li>Females: bidirectional relationship was confirmed for direct and indirect effects</li>
      <li>Significant indirect effect of females' PH on MH, and MH on PH, was smoking, but effect was small</li>
      <li>Surprisingly, smoking directly and negatively predicted MH in females (b = − 0.08 , p = .042)</li>
      <li>Conclusion: smoking may diminish the negative impact of depression and physical illnesses in some individuals, likely females</li>
      <li>These findings may provide another explanation for the rationale behind smoking despite its known harmful effects</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Column 2: Participants/Measures/Procedure -->
  <div class="col">
    <div class="section-title">PARTICIPANTS AND SETTING</div>
    <p>Data from the New Immigrant Survey (NIS) that gathered information between 2003 and 2004</p>
    <p>Sampling was nationally representative, based on the electronic records of 12,500 adults applying for residency</p>
    <p>Rate of response to the NIS – 68.6% (N = 8,573)</p>
    <p>Nearly 12% reported feeling sad, blue, or depressed</p>
    <p>Study used a depression-reported subsample (n = 1,008)</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Females represented 61.4% (n = 619)</li>
      <li>Status: most participants were married (n = 605; 60%)</li>
      <li>Educational attainment range: 0 to 28 years</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="section-title">MEASURES AND INSTRUMENTATION</div>
    <p><em>Mental Health (MH)</em> – sum of "yes" replies to questions from the World Health Organization's Composite International Diagnostic Interview Short Form (CIDI-SF), measuring depression, coded as 1</p>
    <p><em>Physical Health (PH)</em> – sum of "yes" replies to questions on diagnoses (e.g., diabetes, cancer, heart problems, stroke) and functional impairments (e.g., wearing hearing aid, pain, confined to bed), coded as 1</p>
    <p><em>Lifestyle Choices</em> – quantified replies on frequency of exercising, (vigorous and light), and number of cigarettes, smoked current and past</p>
    <p><em>Social Engagement</em> – quantified replies to the question about number of people residing with the immigrant</p>
    <p><em>Gender</em> – dichotomous, with 0 for male, 1 for female</p>
    <p><em>Age</em> – ordinal variable denoting 10 age groups, from youngest to oldest, with 5 year-intervals</p>

    <div class="section-title">PROCEDURE AND DATA ANALYSIS</div>
    <p>All analyses were performed on SPSS, version 24</p>
    <p>Missing values uncovered for 9 case reporting MH; these cases were removed</p>
    <p>Models were tested for assumptions</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Data was positively skewed toward low/moderate PH and MH values but consistent with previous findings</li>
      <li>Bootstrapping n = 1,000 was performed to control for sampling and distributional biases</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Parallel mediation was tested via Model 4 of PROCESS macro, v. 3.0 for SPSS, and moderation via Model 3</p>
    <p>All results reported at 95% CI,  p &lt; .001</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Column 3: Results (part 1) -->
  <div class="col">
    <div class="section-title">RESULTS</div>
    <div class="sub-heading">General Sample</div>
    <ul>
      <li>MH significantly predicted PH (b = 0.16, p &lt; .001); mediational model explained 20.5% of PH variance, with age as a covariate (b = 0.35, p &lt; .001).</li>
      <li>Significant indirect effect of MH on PH via smoking ( b = −0.04), when depression was at ≥ 5symptoms</li>
      <li>This mediation represented a relatively small but inverse effect: current smoking, k² = -.032, [ − 0.63, − 0.06], and past smoking, k² = − .017, [− 0.04, −0.01].</li>
      <li>Direct effect of PH on MH (b = 0.19, p &lt; .001) was significant but mediation was not found</li>
      <li>Direct effect of smoking on PH was adverse</li>
      <li>Exercise and social engagement – no effect on PH-MH</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="fig-caption">Figure 1.<br><em>Symptoms and Habits Across Age Groups (N 1,0008)</em></div>
    <div class="placeholder" style="width:100%; height:220px;">[Chart: line graph of MH, PH, current smoking, vigorous/light exercise across age groups]</div>
    <div class="fig-note">Note: MH – mean of depression, PH – mean of physical conditions</div>

    <div class="sub-heading">Males</div>
    <ul>
      <li>PH and MH had no effect on each other, although</li>
      <li>Current smoking reduced the effect of MH on PH, at MH = 4 symptoms (b = − 0.04)</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="sub-heading">Females I</div>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>PH model</strong> predicted 6% of MH variance (p &lt; .001)</li>
      <li>PH direct effect on MH, b = 0.26, [0.17, 0.35], p &lt;.001</li>
      <li>Current smoking – direct inverse effect on MH, that was significant (b = − 0.08 , [− 0.16, − 0.01], p = .042)</li>
      <li>Mediation: current smoking and current smoking/ social engagement was in the same direction (b = −0.02)</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Column 4: Results II / Discussion / Table -->
  <div class="col">
    <div class="section-title">RESULTS</div>
    <div class="sub-heading">Females II</div>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>MH Model</strong> predicted 22.5% of PH variance (p &lt; .001)</li>
      <li>Clinical levels of depression (MH = 7) contributed 77%</li>
      <li>Current smoking reduced effects of depression on health, at low (MH =1) and clinical (MH = 7) levels ( b = −0.04)</li>
      <li>Past smoking had similar effect but mediation emerged at moderate (MH = 4) and clinical levels of MH ( b = −0.03)</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="section-title">DISCUSSION</div>
    <p>Rates of clinical depression: 24% males 31% females</p>
    <p>Rates of health conditions were comparable</p>
    <p>Significant gender differences for PH-MH relationship were unexpected, given similar distributions of mean scores (Table 1) and existing research</p>
    <p>Based on findings: smoking, indeed, directly predicts higher rates of health problems, for both genders</p>
    <p>However, smoking may mediate negative effects of physical health on women's mental state</p>
    <p>In this sample, smoking appeared to diminish the adverse effect of depression on females' physical health</p>
    <p>In Robinson et al. (2007), females were more responsive to the hedonic properties of nicotine than males</p>
    <p>Antidepressant effect of nicotine (lowering of serotonin and increasing of dopamine) was found in animal studies</p>
    <p>Limitations: other mediators and covariates exist; effect of age not considered; alternative explanation for mediation</p>

